        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The <code>defaultMode</code> can only be specified at the projected level and not for each volume source. However, as illustrated above, you can explicitly set the <code>mode</code> for each individual projection.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold">Pathing Considerations</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <ul><li>Collisions Between Keys when Configured Paths are Identical. If you configure any keys with the same path, the pod spec will not be accepted as valid. In the following example, the specified path for <code>mysecret</code> and <code>myconfigmap</code> are the same:</li></ul>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: volume-test\nspec:\n  securityContext:\n    runAsNonRoot: true\n    seccompProfile:\n      type: RuntimeDefault\n  containers:\n  - name: container-test\n    image: busybox\n    volumeMounts:\n    - name: all-in-one\n      mountPath: \"/projected-volume\"\n      readOnly: true\n    securityContext:\n      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n      capabilities:\n        drop: [ALL]\n  volumes:\n  - name: all-in-one\n    projected:\n      sources:\n      - secret:\n          name: mysecret\n          items:\n            - key: username\n              path: my-group/data\n      - configMap:\n          name: myconfigmap\n          items:\n            - key: config\n              path: my-group/data"} lang="yaml" />
      </dd>
      </dl>
      <p>Consider the following situations related to the volume file paths.</p>
      <ul>
      <li>Collisions Between Keys without Configured Paths. The only run-time validation that can occur is when all the paths are known at pod creation, similar to the above scenario. Otherwise, when a conflict occurs the most recent specified resource overwrites anything preceding it (this is true for resources that are updated after pod creation as well).</li>
      <li>Collisions when One Path is Explicit and the Other is Automatically Projected. If there is a collision due to a user-specified path matching data that is automatically projected, the latter resource overwrites anything preceding it as before.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="nodes-containers-projected-volumes-creating_nodes-containers-projected-volumes" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring a projected volume for a Pod<a href="#nodes-containers-projected-volumes-creating_nodes-containers-projected-volumes" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can create projected volumes to map multiple configuration sources, such as secrets and config maps, into a single directory. Projected volumes centralize sensitive information and environment metadata for your applications into a single directory.  </p>
      <p>When creating projected volumes, consider the volume file path situations described in <em>Understanding projected volumes</em>.</p>
      <p>The following example shows how to use a projected volume to mount an existing secret volume source. The steps can be used to create a user name and password secrets from local files. You then create a pod that runs one container, using a projected volume to mount the secrets into the same shared directory.</p>
      <p>The user name and password values can be any valid string that is <strong>base64</strong> encoded.</p>
      <p>The following example shows <code>admin</code> in base64:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ echo -n \"admin\" | base64"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"YWRtaW4="} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>The following example shows the password <code>1f2d1e2e67df</code> in base64:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ echo -n \"1f2d1e2e67df\" | base64"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"MWYyZDFlMmU2N2Rm"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>The following procedure uses a projected volume to mount an existing secret volume source.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create the secret:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a YAML file similar to the following, replacing the password and user information as appropriate:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: mysecret\ntype: Opaque\ndata:\n  pass: MWYyZDFlMmU2N2Rm\n  user: YWRtaW4="} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Use the following command to create the secret:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f <secrets-filename>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f secret.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"secret \"mysecret\" created"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      You can check that the secret was created using the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get secret <secret-name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get secret mysecret"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME       TYPE      DATA      AGE\nmysecret   Opaque    2         17h"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get secret <secret-name> -o yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get secret mysecret -o yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\ndata:\n  pass: MWYyZDFlMmU2N2Rm\n  user: YWRtaW4=\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  creationTimestamp: 2017-05-30T20:21:38Z\n  name: mysecret\n  namespace: default\n  resourceVersion: \"2107\"\n  selfLink: /api/v1/namespaces/default/secrets/mysecret\n  uid: 959e0424-4575-11e7-9f97-fa163e4bd54c\ntype: Opaque"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a pod with a projected volume.
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a YAML file similar to the following, including a <code>volumes</code> section:
      <CodeBlock code={"kind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: test-projected-volume\nspec:\n  securityContext:\n    runAsNonRoot: true\n    seccompProfile:\n      type: RuntimeDefault\n  containers:\n  - name: test-projected-volume\n    image: busybox\n    args:\n    - sleep\n    - \"86400\"\n    volumeMounts:\n    - name: all-in-one\n      mountPath: \"/projected-volume\"\n      readOnly: true\n    securityContext:\n      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n      capabilities:\n        drop: [ALL]\n  volumes:\n  - name: all-in-one\n    projected:\n      sources:\n      - secret:\n          name: <my_secret>"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;my_secret&gt;</code> with the name of the secret you created.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the pod from the configuration file:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f <your_yaml_file>.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f secret-pod.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"pod \"test-projected-volume\" created"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that the pod container is running, and then watch for changes to the pod:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pod <name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pod test-projected-volume"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output should appear similar to the following:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                    READY     STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\ntest-projected-volume   1/1       Running   0          14s"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      In another terminal, use the <code>oc exec</code> command to open a shell to the running container:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc exec -it <pod> <command>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc exec -it test-projected-volume -- /bin/sh"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      In your shell, verify that the <code>projected-volumes</code> directory contains your projected sources:
      <CodeBlock code={"/ # ls"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"bin               home              root              tmp\ndev               proc              run               usr\netc               projected-volume  sys               var"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
